---\
\
THE PROBLEM\
\
Most insurance claims are simple. A small number are genuinely ambiguous, missing information, or fraudulent, and those are the ones that eat an adjuster's day \'97 not because the work is hard, but because separating the merely-flagged from the actually-wrong takes real judgment, every time, at volume. I wanted to build something that does that sorting honestly: surface what's genuinely uncertain, ground every claim in real evidence and real policy language, and leave the decision itself with a person who can be held accountable for it. Not an autopilot. A system that does the reading so a human can do the deciding faster, with better information than they'd have otherwise.\
\
I chose medical claims specifically because it's a domain with real regulatory stakes \'97 ERISA governs exactly how a denial has to be justified \'97 real fraud patterns worth naming correctly, and enough structural complexity (deductibles, network status, benefit caps, linked claims) to make "just ask the model" an obviously wrong approach on its own.\
\
---\
\
WHERE THIS STARTED\
\
I didn't start with medical claims. I started with a question: what does a genuinely agentic AI system look like, built by someone who directs the build rather than hand-writes every line \'97 router, retrieval, confidence, a human still holding the actual decision? I'd already proven a narrower version of that thesis with AUGUR, a single-call accessibility tool. This project needed to prove the harder version: real tool-use routing, real isolated evaluation, a system with actual teeth.\
\
I brainstormed the shape of that system across half a dozen domains before choosing one \'97 a financial-ops copilot routing spend approvals, an access-request system gating internal tool permissions, a staffing copilot balancing coverage gaps. Every one of them shared the same underlying skeleton: a router deciding which of several actions a plain-language question actually needs, a retrieval layer grounding every answer in real source material, a confidence signal that doesn't overstate itself, and a human gate that nothing gets past without. That skeleton was the actual discovery. The domain was a choice about which version of it to build first.\
\
I picked medical claims because it doesn't forgive shortcuts. Financial-ops copilots can get away with soft heuristics. Claims can't \'97 a denial has a real regulatory shape, a real appeals process, real consequences for getting the reasoning wrong. If the architecture held up here, it would hold up anywhere I re-skinned it. I found the actual regulatory bodies that needed to govern this system iteratively, not all at once: ERISA's tiered claims-procedure deadlines first, then the standard cross-agency fraud terminology (phantom billing, upcoding, unbundling \'97 sourced to OIG, DOJ, and CMS's own coding-integrity program, not any one document), then a documented FinCEN red-flag pattern for volume spikes specifically. Naming the system came after the architecture, not before \'97 ClaimsDock, because a dock is where something arrives to be checked before it moves further; Anchor, for the embedded assistant, because an anchor is the thing that keeps an answer tethered to a real source instead of drifting.\
\
---\
\
SYSTEMS THINKING: FOUR LAYERS, ONE SET OF RULES\
\
I designed ClaimsDock as four systems sharing a small number of hard rules, not one AI system with a few features bolted on. A claim moves through all four differently, and that's the actual point:\
\
- An Evaluation Pipeline that reads every incoming claim automatically and produces evidence, a confidence tier, and a recommendation \'97 no human involved yet.\
- A Router and an embedded assistant, Anchor, that an adjuster can ask a question of at any time, in plain language, grounded in the same evidence and the same policy documents the Pipeline used.\
- A Human Gate that sits between any AI output and any real action, backed by its own guardrails.\
- A retrieval layer, shared by both the Pipeline and Anchor, that grounds everything either of them says in real reference documents instead of the model's own memory.\
\
This is cross-layer systems thinking in practice \'97 reasoning about how one shared claim gets treated differently by four functionally distinct pieces of the same system, not about multiple human roles. There's exactly one user here, an adjuster, and the complexity lives entirely in how many different jobs the system itself does around that one person.\
\
The rule that ties all four together, and the one I held to the most strictly through the whole build: calculations stay calculations. Severity, SLA math, coverage math, and the category-to-recommended-action mapping are all deterministic code, never a model's judgment call. I made this decision in the very first planning pass, before any pipeline code existed, because the alternative \'97 asking a model to compute a dollar amount or a deadline \'97 trades a testable function for a probabilistic one with no upside. A model earns its keep reading messy evidence and judging genuinely ambiguous things. It has no business re-deriving arithmetic a spreadsheet does correctly every time.\
\
---\
\
THE EVALUATION PIPELINE: TWO CALLS, NOT ONE\
\
Every claim goes through exactly two model calls, and I designed it that way on purpose.\
\
The first call reads the claim \'97 plus its linked claim, if it's part of a professional/facility pair, plus the billing provider's recent volume history if a spike pattern is relevant \'97 and produces raw evidence and a proposed category: clean, ambiguous, missing data, complex coverage math, or fraudulent. It does not rate its own confidence, and it does not recommend an action.\
\
The second call is shown only that evidence and category \'97 never the first call's own reasoning, never the original claim again \'97 and independently judges a confidence tier before drafting the recommendation. This is a real LLM-as-judge implementation, not the concept named in passing. I split these into two isolated calls specifically to avoid a model grading its own work in the same breath it did the work. A single call that finds a pattern and immediately rates how sure it is of that pattern is checking its own homework. Two calls, with the second one blind to the first one's reasoning, is a genuinely independent judgment instead.\
\
That confidence tier matters more than it might look. Anchor's four tiers \'97 High Confidence, Confident, Suspected, Uncertain \'97 describe how directly the evidence supports a conclusion, not a bare probability score. I made this distinction deliberately after reading research on how people actually respond to disclosed algorithm accuracy: a number invites false precision. A tier tied to the strength of the actual evidence is something a human can reason about the way they'd reason about a colleague's confidence, not a statistic they either trust blindly or ignore.\
\
The claim set grew substantially over the build, from 20 authored claims to 132, covering clean, ambiguous, missing-data, complex-math, and fraud scenarios in a distribution I negotiated deliberately rather than left arbitrary \'97 I cut the ambiguous category from 12 down to 6 partway through, since too many flagged claims dead-ending at "escalate, no other outcome possible" made the demo unsatisfying to actually click through. At that scale, a single Pipeline call per batch stopped being viable \'97 a real, uncached run against the full set timed out past both the model's own practical limits and this project's own request ceiling. I chunked the claim set into batches sized dynamically off the total claim count, with a continuous-cursor distribution algorithm keeping every batch's category mix, and therefore its token cost, roughly even. Getting that distribution right took three real, live-tested attempts: a naive contiguous slice clustered similar claims together and made per-batch variance worse; a binary clean/non-clean split fixed one call's variance while making the other one worse; the actual fix concatenates every category's claims in file order and round-robins a single continuous cursor across the whole set, never resetting between categories \'97 verified with a unit test before spending another live API call testing it. At 132 claims, that's six batches, each with its own retry, after I watched one flaky network timeout on a single batch discard five other batches' already-succeeded, already-paid-for results.\
\
---\
\
GROUNDING: RETRIEVAL, CHUNKING, AND THE HONEST LIMITS OF EMBEDDING SEARCH\
\
Every recommendation ClaimsDock makes traces back to one of three reference documents I wrote myself: a Coverage and Adjudication Policy (deductible logic, network rates, prior-authorization rules, benefit caps \'97 synthetic but structurally realistic), a Fraud-Indicator Reference (standard industry terminology for billing fraud, sourced from OIG enforcement history, DOJ False Claims Act settlements, and CMS's own coding-integrity program, plus one documented FinCEN volume-spike pattern), and a Regulatory Deadline Reference covering ERISA's actual tiered decision deadlines. Anchor and the Pipeline both cite these by name. Nothing gets invented on the fly.\
\
I built the retrieval layer on local, self-hosted embeddings rather than a hosted embedding API, specifically to avoid a second external dependency for a corpus this small doesn't need one for. Each document gets chunked on its own natural section headings, embedded once, and held in a plain in-memory array \'97 no hosted vector database, since at a few dozen chunks total, comparing a query's embedding against every chunk directly is instant, and a hosted database would be solving a scale problem this project doesn't have.\
\
Retrieval quality turned out to be its own real, ongoing tradeoff, not a solved problem I set once and forgot. A live smoke test against genuine questions \'97 not synthetic fixtures \'97 found that the exact chunk containing ERISA's own tiered-deadline table ranked fifth of six results for the one question it was built to answer. The chunk was built almost entirely from raw markdown table syntax, which a sentence-embedding model, trained overwhelmingly on prose, embeds poorly. Flattening the table into plain-language sentences barely moved the ranking, which told me the real problem was structural: mean-pooled embeddings dilute a long chunk's vector toward its average content, letting a short, keyword-dense chunk systematically out-rank a longer one that actually contains the right answer \'97 a genuine, well-documented limitation of embedding search on its own. I fixed it with hybrid scoring, blending embedding similarity at 70% with a lightweight keyword-overlap signal at 30%, which recovered the ERISA chunk to second place. I left the ranking imperfect past that point on purpose. Both Anchor and the Pipeline retrieve several top-ranked chunks for real synthesis, not just the single top result, so a small ranking margin doesn't produce a wrong answer even when the ranking itself isn't perfect. Tuning retrieval further would have chased diminishing returns against a problem the architecture already tolerates.\
\
---\
\
THE ROUTER AND ANCHOR: AN AGENTIC LOOP, NOT A CHATBOT WRAPPER\
\
Anchor is the part of ClaimsDock an adjuster actually talks to \'97 a plain-language question box that can look up a claim, re-analyze it, draft a recommendation, or answer a general policy question, entirely independent of whatever the automatic Pipeline already decided. Underneath it is a real agentic tool-use loop: a Router classifies each question into one of four tools and extracts the right parameters, executes the tool, and can chain a second tool call if the first result implies a follow-up is needed, inside a bounded round budget rather than a fixed one-shot call.\
\
I designed against three distinct, separately testable failure modes, because they're genuinely different problems with different fixes: the Router choosing the wrong tool entirely; choosing the right tool but extracting the wrong parameters (the right kind of analysis, pointed at the wrong claim); and, specific to general policy questions, misjudging whether a question is about one particular claim or nothing in particular. Tool-choice accuracy and parameter-extraction accuracy aren't the same failure mode and don't get fixed the same way \'97 a wrong tool choice is a routing-prompt problem, while a right tool with wrong parameters is usually a context problem, like the Router defaulting to whichever claim happened to be on screen instead of the one actually named in the question. I tested both separately with an adversarial question set built specifically to probe each one, not just the happy path, and caught a real bug on the first live run: a question needing two tools in sequence \'97 claim facts, then a policy fact \'97 had its second tool call silently dropped, because the original round-trip only handled a single dispatch round. I fixed it as a bounded loop instead of assuming every real question resolves in one call, which looks reasonable right up until someone asks a genuinely compound question.\
\
One more find, worth its own beat: the internal claim ID I'd been sending to the model in every Pipeline and Router call wasn't a neutral identifier. It encoded the claim's own ground-truth scenario directly in its text \'97 a fraud-category test claim's ID literally spelled out which fraud type it was authored to represent. I fixed it by generating one opaque, realistic-looking display number per claim, used for two jobs at once: what the UI shows an adjuster, and the only identifier ever sent to or returned by a model call. The real internal ID stays a private join key, never forwarded anywhere a model can read it. A second, separate leak surfaced later in the same vein \'97 a free-text provider note, written for an entirely different purpose, embedded raw internal IDs as prose, and I caught it live when Kimi quoted one back to an adjuster verbatim inside an Anchor answer. Neither leak was in the code I'd deliberately redacted; both were in adjacent surfaces the original fix never touched. It's the kind of gap that's easy to miss once and important to actually go looking for a second time, not just patch and move on.\
\
---\
\
CLAUDE VS. KIMI: A REAL EXPERIMENT, NOT A FEATURE CHECKLIST\
\
I didn't pick a model provider by reputation. I built a real comparison harness and ran both providers against the identical, fixed claim set across repeated matched batches, then designed the metric I actually cared about before trusting either provider's raw accuracy number.\
\
Raw accuracy treats every wrong category as equally bad, and it isn't. A model that gets a claim's category wrong but stays cautious about it still reaches a human for review \'97 the deterministic layer routes anything below a confident tier to a person regardless of category. The dangerous failure is different: a wrong "clean" call paired with a confident tier, which auto-resolves with nobody ever reviewing it. I called this a silent miss and tracked it specifically, since it's the one failure mode this system's own safety architecture can't catch after the fact.\
\
Confirmed across repeated matched batches: Claude averaged 18.4 of 20 claims correct (92%), consistently, across every prompt iteration tested. Kimi's numbers moved as I refined the prompt \'97 and then I found something in my own evaluation harness worth catching before trusting the comparison at all: one of Kimi's silent misses, one that had been dragging its whole rate down, traced back to a mislabeled ground-truth claim in my own test set, not a real model error. I fixed the mislabeling and added one more targeted prompt example. Kimi's corrected result reached 18.6 of 20 (a hair above Claude's own confirmed rate) with a 5% silent-miss rate \'97 nearly matching Claude's own 5%, where it had been running measurably higher before the correction. I'm treating that latest result as promising and not yet fully proven \'97 one corrected batch, not the same repeated-batch confirmation Claude's number has behind it \'97 and I said so plainly rather than round it up early.\
\
The real, unambiguous difference that survived every round of testing is cost: Kimi runs roughly five times cheaper per token than Claude, which is the entire reason this comparison exists in the first place rather than defaulting to one provider. I made three separate provider decisions, not one, and I made each for its own specific reason. The Pipeline runs on Kimi in production \'97 a cost-driven call appropriate to a public prototype with no real patients or dollars on the line, documented explicitly as a different call than an actual production system, with real stakes attached to any accuracy gap, would be permitted to make. Anchor is switchable between both providers from a settings toggle, since its own answer quality was worth testing on its own terms rather than assumed to track the Pipeline's choice. And the Human Gate's Deny guardrail \'97 the single highest-stakes output in the whole system \'97 ships on Kimi permanently, a decision I closed rather than left open, after Kimi proved sufficient through eleven full rounds of live refinement plus a permanent regression suite; I never needed to fall back to Claude there, so I didn't build a fallback path that would never get used.\
\
One difference I noticed hands-on, past the formal metrics: on a round-budget test capped at five tool calls, Claude self-limited to three and explicitly asked to continue, while Kimi completed all five in a single turn \'97 a real architectural difference in how each provider's function-calling handles multiple tool calls per turn, not a quality gap either direction. Kimi's answers inside Anchor were also, if anything, more thorough for the same questions, using real generated tables and bulleted structure more often than Claude, which consistently fell back to prose with the occasional list for the same kind of request. Neither of these was a metric I built a formal test around \'97 just something worth knowing if you're evaluating either model for a task where structured output actually helps a reader.\
\
---\
\
THE HUMAN GATE: WHERE AI OUTPUT MEETS A REAL DECISION\
\
Nothing in ClaimsDock becomes a real action \'97 an approval, a denial, an escalation \'97 without a human clicking to make it happen, and I built two guardrails specifically for the moment that click happens.\
\
The first is deterministic and costs nothing: it compares whatever action an adjuster chooses against the Pipeline's own recommendation and surfaces a soft confirmation, citing the evidence already on file, if the two disagree. It never blocks anyone from proceeding with their own choice \'97 a system that could override a human's own decision would contradict the entire premise the platform rests on. I scoped this check to every action except Approve-with-edit, deliberately: an override on approval doesn't carry a denial's regulatory stakes, and the free mismatch check already covers the accountability moment for a corrected recommendation without needing a second, model-backed layer on top of it.\
\
The second guardrail is where most of the real engineering difficulty in this whole build lived. Denying a claim requires four structured justification fields \'97 the specific reason, the plan or policy provision it violates, any internal standard applied, and what could reverse the decision \'97 and a model-backed check grades that justification's coherence against the claim's own evidence before it's accepted, drafting a better version when it falls short. I refined this single guardrail across eleven documented rounds of real, live failure: a wrong document cited, a fabricated policy provision, a genuine gap in my own reference corpus that no amount of prompt tuning could paper over, a retrieval-recall problem where the same correct citation ranked differently depending on how clinically-worded a claim's evidence happened to read, and \'97 twice \'97 two of my own guardrail rules directly contradicting each other on different claims.\
\
The actual fix, once I stepped back far enough to see it, wasn't another prompt patch. One of the four fields was never a genuine judgment call in the first place: a denial for fraud only ever follows one specific confidence path, so the correct policy citation for that field is always the same fixed text. I removed it from judgment entirely rather than keep asking a model to independently rediscover an answer that never varied. I built a permanent regression suite directly from that saga's own lesson \'97 every one of those eleven rounds had been checked only against the single claim that had just failed, with nothing verifying a fix didn't silently break a claim that had already passed, and the suite caught exactly that kind of regression on its very first run.\
\
Every status change, override, escalation, and denial writes to an audit log with who, when, and why \'97 a flat, queryable record specifically so a real question like "show me every claim approved in March" is answerable directly, not reconstructed after the fact. A claim's current status is always just its latest log entry, so there's no separate status value that could quietly drift out of sync with the record of how it got there.\
\
---\
\
THE INTERFACE: SIX REAL DESIGN COMBINATIONS, ONE TOKEN SYSTEM\
\
I designed ClaimsDock's interface myself, starting from a plain layout I sketched early \'97 a masthead, a claims table, and a side panel of mini-cards for the assistant \'97 long before any of the backend architecture above existed as running code. That rough shape became the seed for five real style directions, explored side by side rather than picked from a single default. I narrowed those five to three, and I was deliberate about what narrowing meant: not picking a winner and discarding the rest, but keeping the three that each carried a genuinely different point of view on the same underlying data, not just a different palette on the same layout.\
\
Ledger reads dense and tabular, built for a high-volume queue where information density is the point. Clinical is serif-forward and precision-first, closer to how an actual clinical record reads. Field is warmer and more approachable, built for a less adversarial read of the same claims. Each of the three ships in both light and dark \'97 six real combinations total \'97 driven by a single token architecture rather than six separately hand-maintained copies. A style-and-brightness pair is one entry in a shared color and type system, so adding a seventh combination later means adding one token block, not rewriting six sets of component styles by hand.\
\
I held the whole system to WCAG AAA contrast, the strictest tier, at 7:1, and checked it computationally rather than eyeballing it \'97 which caught three real failures I wouldn't have caught by looking, accent text against its own ink color dropping as low as 4.77:1 in Clinical light. I fixed all three at the primitive-token level rather than patching the individual components that happened to expose them, so the fix propagated to every place that token gets used at once.\
\
Wiring the real design into working React surfaced genuine engineering bugs, not just visual polish work. A shared fade-in and fade-out animation was starting its exit sequence from inside a React effect, which only runs after the browser has already committed a frame \'97 meaning a modal could sit fully removed from the page for one real paint before the effect ever got a chance to fade it back in, and claims with more content on them made that gap far more likely to actually be seen. I traced it to React's own commit timing and fixed it by deriving the transition synchronously during render instead of reacting to it after the fact. Separately, a status-and-severity legend popover was getting clipped at the bottom of a short table \'97 not a popover bug at all, but a table container that had set horizontal scrolling without setting vertical scrolling explicitly, which CSS quietly resolves to the same clipping behavior by default. I fixed it by rendering the popover through a portal instead of fighting the table's own overflow rules.\
\
None of this was a single generation I approved and moved on from. The interface went through real, hands-on iteration over a genuinely large amount of work \'97 the kind that doesn't show up as a line in a spec document, but is exactly the layer an adjuster actually spends their whole day looking at.\
\
---\
\
RESEARCH: WHY DENISE'S SKEPTICISM ISN'T A CHARACTER QUIRK\
\
I built ClaimsDock's interface around a persona, Denise Marsh \'97 a senior claims adjuster nine years into the field, the kind who gets routed the genuinely disputed cases rather than the routine ones. Her caution toward automated recommendations isn't invented color. It's grounded in real, peer-reviewed research, and that research shaped decisions I made in the product itself, not just the persona.\
\
A key tension I designed against is well-documented: people trust algorithms readily on analytical tasks, but that trust drops sharply the moment they see the algorithm make a mistake \'97 more sharply than the same mistake made by a human \'97 and they lean back on their own judgment afterward. Researchers call this algorithm aversion, and its boundaries are studied too: letting people edit an algorithm's output, rather than just accept or reject it, measurably softens the effect. That finding is a direct line to the Human Gate's "Approve with edit" path, which exists specifically so an adjuster never has to reject a correct finding outright just because one number in it is wrong.\
\
One finding changed how I thought about Anchor's confidence tiers specifically: reliance tracks an algorithm's stated accuracy right up until it's actually wrong more often than it said it would be, at which point trust doesn't just decline, it collapses. That's a much narrower target than "show a confidence score." It means Anchor's tiers have to be genuinely accurate, not just present \'97 an honest "Confident" that turns out wrong, or a "Suspected" that turns out right more often than not, is worse for trust than showing no tier at all, which is exactly why I built the tiers around how directly the evidence supports a conclusion rather than a number invented to look precise.\
\
The same research shaped the Human Gate a second way: people aren't blanket-trusting or blanket-rejecting an algorithm's suggestions. They lean on the correct ones more than the incorrect ones, meaning they're actively curating a recommendation, not just accepting it. Anchor's citation-plus-disclaimer pattern is my own version of the well-timed nudge that same research points toward \'97 not proof it works, just a design decision built on real evidence about how people actually calibrate trust, rather than a guess.\
\
The most uncomfortable finding, and the one I take most seriously, challenges an assumption I might otherwise have made by default: that putting a human in the approval path guarantees real oversight on its own. Research testing that assumption directly finds it doesn't reliably hold \'97 people accepted AI-generated advice even when told the AI's training data was unknown or presumably biased, and even when explicitly warned to be skeptical of it. A human checkpoint can become a rubber stamp if the human isn't actually made to engage with the decision. That finding is the entire reason denying a claim in ClaimsDock isn't a single click. It requires the four-field justification described above, checked for real coherence against the claim's own evidence \'97 catching cases where a stated reason cited the wrong fraud category even though the underlying denial itself was correct. The goal was never just a human in the loop. It's a human who has to actually do something in that loop.\
\
---\
\
PRODUCTION: WHAT ONLY A REAL DEPLOYMENT ACTUALLY TESTS\
\
I built and tested every piece of ClaimsDock locally before deploying it, and the deployment itself still surfaced three real bugs, in order, none of which had ever shown up in months of local development. That gap is the whole reason a real deployment is worth doing rather than skipping \'97 a genuinely different runtime environment, stateless functions, a read-only filesystem, real cold starts, tests things local development structurally can't.\
\
A typo'd environment variable surfaced first, as a credential error from the wrong SDK entirely \'97 Kimi's client silently falls back to checking for an OpenAI key when its own resolves empty, which took a real log dive to trace back to a one-character typo. Second, and harder to find: the local embedding library's native binary wasn't reaching the deployed serverless function at all, since Next.js's automatic file-tracing doesn't reliably catch a shared library loaded dynamically at runtime instead of imported statically \'97 my first fix wasn't sufficient on its own, confirmed live, and I had to add an explicit include for the exact routes that needed it before it actually worked. Third, once retrieval got past that: the same library's default cache directory lives inside a folder that's read-only on Vercel at runtime, redirected to the one writable path a serverless function actually has. None of these are exotic bugs. They're exactly the kind of environment-specific gap that never shows up until you actually ship, which is the whole argument for shipping early and for real rather than treating "it works on my machine" as the finish line.\
\
The Pipeline's own results are cached per calendar week and shared across every visitor, since the output depends only on claim data, not on who's asking \'97 meaning only the first visitor after a weekly cache rollover pays for a live run, and I closed even that gap with a scheduled job that pre-warms the cache before real traffic arrives.\
\
---\
\
WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES\
\
ClaimsDock is a real, deployed system, not a demo that only works in a controlled walkthrough. I directed and reviewed the entire build through Claude Code, treating it the way I'd direct an engineering team \'97 deciding architecture on paper before code exists, reviewing every real bug and every reversed decision, catching my own assumptions before they became shipped mistakes.\
\
It reflects the way I actually approach a build: decide the architecture before writing code, treat a model as the tool for genuine judgment calls and deterministic code as the tool for everything else, test a real tradeoff instead of assuming an answer, and verify a fix against the environment it actually has to survive in. A condensed, phase-by-phase build log, linked separately, covers the full process in more depth than belongs here \'97 every real bug, every reversed decision, and the reasoning behind each one.\
